A backslash escape inside a list item survives, whether or not the item
opens with a checkbox.

The children of a list item are whole blocks, so each one comes back
through the CommonMark writer with its backslashes. An escape that is
dropped turns the author's text back into markup: `\*not emphasis\*`
becomes emphasis, and `\&amp;` becomes a live entity that renders as a
bare ampersand, which still LOOKS right in a terminal.

The last item is the one to watch. The box is read from the first bracket
pair on the line, so an ESCAPED `\[x\]` later in the prose must neither be
mistaken for the box nor tick the real one.
